\title{Euler's Two-Squares Route, Fully Priced:\\
Unconditional Extraction, an Exact Eligibility Class,\\
and a Quartic Search Barrier}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{30 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Euler's factorisation method converts two essentially distinct representations of an integer $N$ as a sum of two squares, $N = a^2+b^2 = c^2+d^2$, into a nontrivial divisor by a single greatest-common-divisor computation. We give a complete accounting of the method: its algebraic core, its exact domain of applicability, and an unconditional lower bound on the cost of its input.

\emph{Extraction.} We prove $1 < \gcd(ad-bc, N) < N$ whenever the two representations are essentially distinct, with no primality, smoothness, genericity or distributional hypothesis, and with the parts allowed to lie on the closed cone $a,b,c,d \ge 0$. The proof combines the two Brahmagupta--Fibonacci identities with a rigidity lemma that is the integral equality case of Cauchy--Schwarz. Corollaries include the essential uniqueness of the two-squares representation of a prime, the identity $\gcd(ad-bc,N)\gcd(ad+bc,N)=pq$ for semiprimes, and, on the canonical Brahmagupta pair, the exact identities $AD-BC = 2efq$ and $AD+BC = 2ghp$, which pin the two extracted divisors to $q$ and $p$.

\emph{Eligibility.} For $N=pq$ with $p \neq q$ odd primes, two essentially distinct representations exist if and only if $p \equiv q \equiv 1 \pmod 4$, and then there are exactly two. The upper bound follows from an injective class map into $\{0,1\}^2$, whose coordinates we identify with divisibility by the conjugate Gaussian primes above $p$ and above $q$. The eligible fraction of pairs of odd primes is therefore exactly $1/4$.

\emph{Cost.} We prove an unconditional \emph{quartic barrier}: if a search bound $t$ has reached the smaller part of each of two essentially distinct representations of $N$, then $2N < t^4$. A gap-refined form gives $2k^2N < c^4$ when the large parts differ by $k$, whence three representations force $8N < a_3^4$. Combined with the exact criterion $(q-p)^2 < 4(p+q)$ for Fermat's difference-of-squares scan to halt on its first trial, this yields: on a balanced eligible semiprime, Fermat succeeds in one step while any representation search that collects both representations must run past $(2N)^{1/4}$, twice. The constant-factor loss of the representation route is forced, not an artefact of sampling.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} sum of two squares; Euler factorisation; Brahmagupta--Fibonacci identity; Gaussian integers; Fermat's difference of squares; integer factorisation; search lower bound.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The method}

Euler observed that a number possessing two genuinely different decompositions into a sum of two squares reveals its factorisation immediately. From
\[
N = a^2+b^2 = c^2+d^2
\]
compute $\gcd(ad-bc,\,N)$.

For $N = 221$ we have $221 = 5^2+14^2 = 10^2+11^2$, so $ad-bc = 5\cdot 11 - 14\cdot 10 = -85$ and $\gcd(85,221) = 17$; the complementary cross-term $ad+bc = 195$ gives $\gcd(195,221)=13$, and $221 = 13\cdot 17$.

Conceptually the cross-term is an imaginary part. Identify representations with Gaussian integers $z_1 = a+bi$ and $z_2 = c+di$ of norm $N$; then
\[
z_1\overline{z_2} = (ac+bd) + (ad-bc)\,i,
\]
so $ad-bc = \Imm(z_1\overline{z_2})$, $ac+bd = \Ree(z_1\overline{z_2})$ and $|z_1\overline{z_2}| = N$. The method is a statement about two lattice points on the circle of radius $\sqrt N$.

\subsection{What is at stake}

An honest evaluation of a factorisation method has three faces: \emph{eligibility} (on what fraction of inputs is it defined?), \emph{correctness} (when defined, does the extraction succeed?), and \emph{cost} (what does producing the input cost, against a competitor?). We settle all three unconditionally.

Section~\ref{sec:extract} proves the extraction theorem in maximal generality; Section~\ref{sec:elig} determines the eligibility class exactly and identifies its combinatorics with Gaussian splitting; Section~\ref{sec:det} makes the extracted divisor deterministic; Section~\ref{sec:fermat} analyses the competing Fermat scan exactly; Section~\ref{sec:barrier} proves the quartic search barrier and assembles the comparison; Sections~\ref{sec:num}--\ref{sec:future} discuss and conclude.

\subsection{Conventions}

All variables denote integers unless stated. A \emph{representation} of $N$ is a pair $(a,b)$ with $a^2+b^2=N$; it is \emph{positive} if $a,b>0$ and \emph{normalised} if $0<a\le b$. Two representations $(a,b)$, $(c,d)$ are \emph{essentially distinct} if
\[
\lnot(c=a \wedge d=b) \quad\text{and}\quad \lnot(d=a \wedge c=b),
\]
i.e.\ they differ as unordered pairs. Write $r_2(n)$ for the number of ordered integer pairs $(x,y)$, signs and zeros included, with $x^2+y^2=n$.

\section{The two Brahmagupta--Fibonacci identities}

\begin{lemma}[Subtractive branch]\label{lem:bf1}
$(a^2+b^2)(c^2+d^2) = (ac+bd)^2 + (ad-bc)^2.$
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Additive branch]\label{lem:bf2}
$(a^2+b^2)(c^2+d^2) = (ac-bd)^2 + (ad+bc)^2.$
\end{lemma}

Both are verified by expansion; they are the real and imaginary parts of the multiplicativity of the Gaussian norm applied to $z_1\overline{z_2}$ and $z_1z_2$.

\begin{lemma}[Cross-product divisibility]\label{lem:crossdvd}
If $c^2+d^2 = a^2+b^2$ then $(a^2+b^2) \mid (ad-bc)(ad+bc)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$(ad-bc)(ad+bc) = a^2d^2 - b^2c^2 = a^2(c^2+d^2) - c^2(a^2+b^2) = (a^2-c^2)(a^2+b^2)$, using $c^2+d^2 = a^2+b^2$ in the middle step.
\end{proof}

\section{Unconditional extraction}\label{sec:extract}

\subsection{Rigidity}

\begin{lemma}[Rigidity; integral equality case of Cauchy--Schwarz]\label{lem:rigid}
Let $a,b,c,d \in \Z$ with $a^2+b^2>0$. If $ad = bc$ and $ac+bd = a^2+b^2$, then $c=a$ and $d=b$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The identity $a(ac+bd) - b(ad-bc) = c(a^2+b^2)$ combined with the two hypotheses gives $a(a^2+b^2) = c(a^2+b^2)$; cancelling the nonzero factor yields $c=a$. If $b \neq 0$, substituting $c=a$ into $ac+bd = a^2+b^2$ gives $bd = b^2$, hence $d=b$. If $b=0$ then $a \neq 0$ and $ad = bc = 0$ forces $d = 0 = b$.
\end{proof}

Geometrically: $ad-bc=0$ says $(a,b)\parallel(c,d)$, while $ac+bd = |(a,b)|^2$ says the projection of $(c,d)$ on $(a,b)$ is exactly $(a,b)$. Nothing is assumed about $c^2+d^2$.

\subsection{The two failure modes}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:notdvd}
Let $a,b,c,d>0$ with $c^2+d^2 = a^2+b^2 =: N$ and $(c,d) \neq (a,b)$. Then $N \nmid (ad-bc)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:bf1} with equal norms, $(ac+bd)^2 + (ad-bc)^2 = N^2$. Suppose $ad-bc = Nk$. Then $(ac+bd)^2 = N^2(1-k^2)$; since $ac+bd>0$ we get $1-k^2>0$, so $k=0$. Hence $ad=bc$ and $(ac+bd)^2 = N^2$ with $ac+bd>0$, so $ac+bd = N = a^2+b^2$. Lemma~\ref{lem:rigid} gives $(c,d)=(a,b)$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:notdvdadd}
Under the same hypotheses, if $(d,c) \neq (a,b)$ then $N \nmid (ad+bc)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:bf2}, $(ac-bd)^2 + (ad+bc)^2 = N^2$, so $0 < ad+bc \le N$. A divisibility $N \mid ad+bc$ therefore forces $ad+bc = N$ and then $ac-bd=0$. Applying Lemma~\ref{lem:rigid} after swapping $c \leftrightarrow d$ yields $(d,c)=(a,b)$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:notcop}
Under the same hypotheses, if $(d,c)\neq(a,b)$ then $\gcd(ad-bc,N)>1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
If $ad-bc$ were coprime to $N$, Lemma~\ref{lem:crossdvd} would give $N \mid (ad+bc)$, contradicting Proposition~\ref{prop:notdvdadd}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The extraction theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Euler's Extraction Theorem]\label{thm:extract}
Let $a,b,c,d$ be positive integers with $a^2+b^2 = c^2+d^2 = N$, the two representations essentially distinct. Then
\[
1 < \gcd(ad-bc,\,N) < N .
\]
In particular Euler's combination step always returns a proper nontrivial divisor of $N$, with no primality, smoothness, genericity or distributional hypothesis.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Put $g = \gcd(ad-bc,N)$, so $g \mid N$ and $g \mid ad-bc$. If $g=0$ then $N=0$, excluded. If $g=1$ then $ad-bc$ is coprime to $N$, contradicting Corollary~\ref{cor:notcop}. If $g=N$ then $N \mid ad-bc$, contradicting Proposition~\ref{prop:notdvd}.
\end{proof}

The same argument on the additive branch gives $1 < \gcd(ad+bc,N) < N$.

\begin{theorem}[Extraction on the closed cone]\label{thm:cone}
Theorem~\ref{thm:extract} remains true with hypotheses weakened to $a,b,c,d \ge 0$ and $N = a^2+b^2>0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Only $ac+bd>0$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:notdvd}) and $ad+bc>0$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:notdvdadd}) used strict positivity, and both survive on the boundary. If $ac+bd=0$ with all parts $\ge 0$ then $ac = bd = 0$, so the vectors are supported on complementary coordinates: $a=d=0$ forces $c^2=b^2$, i.e.\ $(d,c)=(a,b)$; $c=b=0$ forces $d^2=a^2$, likewise; $a=b=0$ or $c=d=0$ contradicts $N>0$. The argument for $ad+bc$ is the mirror image.
\end{proof}

Thus $25 = 5^2+0^2 = 3^2+4^2$ is covered: $\gcd(5\cdot 4 - 0\cdot 3, 25) = 5$.

\subsection{Immediate corollaries}

\begin{corollary}[Uniqueness for primes]
A prime $p$ has at most one representation as a sum of two positive squares, up to order.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Two essentially distinct representations would, by Theorem~\ref{thm:extract}, produce a divisor $g$ of $p$ with $1<g<p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Semiprime extraction]\label{cor:semi}
If $N = pq$ with $p,q$ prime and $a^2+b^2 = c^2+d^2 = N$ are essentially distinct positive representations, then $\gcd(ad-bc,N) \in \{p,q\}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Both gcds recover the factorisation]\label{thm:pair}
If moreover $p \neq q$, then $\gcd(ad-bc,N)\cdot\gcd(ad+bc,N) = pq$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both gcds lie in $\{p,q\}$ by Corollary~\ref{cor:semi} and its additive analogue. By Lemma~\ref{lem:crossdvd}, $N$ divides the product of the cross-terms, so each of $p$ and $q$ divides one of them and hence one of the gcds. If both gcds were $p$, then $q \mid p$; contradiction. Symmetrically for $q$.
\end{proof}

\section{The eligibility class}\label{sec:elig}

\subsection{The negative half}

\begin{theorem}[Obstruction at $3 \bmod 4$]\label{thm:obstr}
Let $r$ be prime with $r \equiv 3 \pmod 4$, and $n$ a positive integer with $r \mid n$, $r^2 \nmid n$. Then $n$ is not a sum of two integer squares.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $a^2+b^2 = n$ then $a^2 \equiv -b^2 \pmod r$. If $r \nmid b$ then $-1$ would be a quadratic residue mod $r$, impossible for $r\equiv 3\pmod 4$. Hence $r \mid b$, then $r \mid a$, so $r^2 \mid n$.
\end{proof}

For semiprimes $N=pq$ with $p\neq q$ odd, Theorem~\ref{thm:obstr} annihilates the cells $(1,3)$, $(3,1)$, $(3,3)$: those $N$ have \emph{no} representations at all.

\subsection{The class map}

Fix distinct primes $p \equiv q \equiv 1 \pmod 4$ and representations $p = e^2+f^2$, $q = g^2+h^2$ with $e,f,g,h>0$; note $e \neq f$ and $g \neq h$ since $p,q$ are odd.

\begin{lemma}[The bit is defined]\label{lem:bit}
Let $a^2+b^2 = pq$. Then $p$ divides exactly one of $af-be$ and $af+be$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Existence: $(af-be)(af+be) = f^2(a^2+b^2) - b^2(e^2+f^2) = f^2pq - b^2p$, a multiple of $p$, so the prime $p$ divides one factor. Exclusivity: if it divided both it would divide $2af$ and $2be$; $p$ is odd and divides neither $e$ nor $f$ (else $p^2 \mid e^2+f^2 = p$), so $p \mid a$ and $p \mid b$, whence $p^2 \mid pq$ and $p \mid q$, contradicting $p \neq q$.
\end{proof}

Define the \emph{class map}
\[
\chi(a,b) = \bigl([\,p \mid af-be\,],\ [\,q \mid ah-bg\,]\bigr) \in \{0,1\}^2 .
\]

\begin{lemma}[Injectivity]\label{lem:inj}
If two positive representations of $pq$ have the same class, they are equal.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Equality of the first coordinate forces $p \mid a_1b_2 - a_2b_1$ (eliminate $(e,f)$ between the two congruences); similarly $q$ divides it, hence $N = pq$ does. Proposition~\ref{prop:notdvd} then forces the two representations to coincide.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exactly two representations]\label{thm:two}
Let $p \neq q$ be primes with $p \equiv q \equiv 1 \pmod 4$. Then $pq$ has exactly two essentially distinct representations as a sum of two positive squares. With $p=e^2+f^2$, $q=g^2+h^2$ these are
\[
A = eg+fh,\quad B = |eh-fg| \qquad\text{and}\qquad C = |eg-fh|,\quad D = eh+fg,
\]
and the four ordered positive representations are exactly $(A,B),(B,A),(C,D),(D,C)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Lemmas~\ref{lem:bf1}--\ref{lem:bf2} give $A^2+B^2 = C^2+D^2 = pq$; positivity of all parts and essential distinctness follow from $e\neq f$, $g\neq h$, $p\neq q$ (in particular $pq$ is not a square). This gives at least four ordered representations; Lemma~\ref{lem:inj} caps the number at $|\{0,1\}^2| = 4$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The dichotomy]\label{thm:dich}
Let $p \neq q$ be odd primes. Then $pq$ admits two essentially distinct representations as a sum of two positive squares if and only if $p \equiv q \equiv 1 \pmod 4$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) Theorem~\ref{thm:two}. ($\Rightarrow$) If say $q \equiv 3 \pmod 4$ then $q \mid pq$, $q^2 \nmid pq$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:obstr} gives no representation at all.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Eligible density]\label{cor:dens}
Primes $\equiv 1$ and $\equiv 3 \bmod 4$ have equal density $1/2$ among odd primes; hence for two independently drawn odd primes the probability that $pq$ is eligible is exactly $1/4$, and conditional on eligibility the representation count is exactly $2$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{The class bit is a Gaussian divisibility}

\begin{theorem}[Bridge to the Gaussian integers]\label{thm:gauss}
Let $p$ be prime with $p = e^2+f^2$, and let $a^2+b^2 = pq$. Then
\[
(e+fi) \mid (a+bi) \ \text{ in }\ \Z[i] \iff p \mid af-be .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) If $a+bi = (e+fi)(u+vi)$ then $a = eu-fv$, $b = ev+fu$, so $af-be = -v(e^2+f^2) = -vp$.

($\Leftarrow$) Let $af-be = pv'$. From $(ae+bf)^2 + (af-be)^2 = (a^2+b^2)(e^2+f^2) = p^2q$ we get $(ae+bf)^2 = p^2(q-v'^2)$, so $p \mid ae+bf$, say $ae+bf = pu$. Then
\[
pa = a(e^2+f^2) = e(ae+bf)+f(af-be) = p(eu+fv'),
\]
\[
pb = b(e^2+f^2) = f(ae+bf)-e(af-be) = p(fu-ev'),
\]
so $a+bi = (e+fi)(u-v'i)$.
\end{proof}

Replacing $f$ by $-f$ gives $(e-fi) \mid (a+bi) \iff p \mid af+be$.

\begin{corollary}[Frobenius dichotomy]\label{cor:frob}
For distinct primes $p \equiv 1 \pmod 4$ and $q$, and any representation $a^2+b^2 = pq$, exactly one of the conjugate Gaussian primes $e \pm fi$ above $p$ divides $a+bi$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Gaussian splitting]
If $p \mid af-be$ then $a+bi = (e+fi)\,w$ for some $w \in \Z[i]$ with $\N(w) = q$.
\end{corollary}

Thus the elementary two-bit argument of Theorem~\ref{thm:two} is a pair of independent choices of Gaussian prime above $p$ and above $q$ --- the structural reason the count is a power of two.

\subsection{Cross-check: the classical counting function}

The count is consistent with $r_2(n) = 4\,(d_1(n)-d_3(n))$, $d_j$ counting divisors $\equiv j \bmod 4$. For an eligible semiprime $N=pq$ all four divisors $1,p,q,pq$ are $\equiv 1 \bmod 4$, so $r_2 = 16$; each unordered representation with distinct positive parts accounts for $8$ signed-and-ordered solutions, giving $16/8 = 2$. For $N=221$: $r_2(221)=16$, representations $\{5,14\}$ and $\{10,11\}$.

\section{Determinism: which prime comes out}\label{sec:det}

Keep $p=e^2+f^2$, $q=g^2+h^2$, and set
\[
A = eg+fh,\quad B = eh-fg,\quad C = eg-fh,\quad D = eh+fg .
\]

\begin{lemma}[Exact factorisation of the cross-terms]\label{lem:exactcross}
\[
AD - BC = 2efq, \qquad AD+BC = 2ghp .
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Both are polynomial identities:
$(eg+fh)(eh+fg) - (eh-fg)(eg-fh) = 2ef(g^2+h^2)$ and
$(eg+fh)(eh+fg) + (eh-fg)(eg-fh) = 2gh(e^2+f^2)$. Expand and compare.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Deterministic extraction]\label{thm:determ}
Let $p,q$ be odd primes with $p = e^2+f^2$, $q = g^2+h^2$, $N = pq$. Then
\[
\gcd(AD-BC,\,N) = q, \qquad \gcd(AD+BC,\,N) = p .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:exactcross}, $q \mid AD-BC$, so the first gcd is $q$ or $pq$; it is not $pq$ since that requires $p \mid 2ef$, and the odd prime $p = e^2+f^2$ divides neither $2$, nor $e$, nor $f$. The additive branch is symmetric.
\end{proof}

So the subtractive cross-term always returns the prime whose representation was \emph{not} consumed by the twist. With normalised non-negative parts, $|B||C| = |BC|$, and which prime emerges is decided purely by the sign of $BC = (eh-fg)(eg-fh)$: positive yields $q$, negative yields $p$.

\begin{lemma}[The twisted parts are never both small]\label{lem:twisted}
With $e,f,g,h>0$, $e\neq f$, $g\neq h$,
\[
B^2+C^2 = pq - 4efgh \ \ge\ p+q-1 .
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The identity $B^2+C^2 = (e^2+f^2)(g^2+h^2)-4efgh$ is polynomial. From $(e-f)^2 \ge 1$ we get $4ef \le 2p-2$, and likewise $4gh \le 2q-2$; multiplying, $16efgh \le (2p-2)(2q-2)$, hence $pq - 4efgh \ge pq - (p-1)(q-1) = p+q-1$.
\end{proof}

Hence $\max(|B|,|C|) \ge \sqrt{(p+q-1)/2}$: the second representation always sits far from the axes.

\section{The competitor: Fermat's difference-of-squares scan}\label{sec:fermat}

Fermat's method tries $s = \lceil\sqrt N\rceil, \lceil\sqrt N\rceil+1,\dots$ until $s^2-N$ is a perfect square. For $N = pq$ with $p<q$ odd, write $p+q = 2u$, $q = p+2v$ with $v>0$.

\begin{lemma}[Correctness and termination point]\label{lem:fid}
$u^2 = pq+v^2$; hence the scan terminates exactly at $s=u$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$u = p+v$, so $u^2 = p^2+2pv+v^2 = p(p+2v)+v^2 = pq+v^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Exact integer-square-root criterion]\label{lem:sqrtcrit}
If $N+t^2 = (w+1)^2$ with $t>0$, then $\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor = w \iff t^2 < 2(w+1)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$N < (w+1)^2$ always gives $\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor \le w$; and $\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor \ge w \iff w^2 \le (w+1)^2-t^2 \iff t^2 \le 2w+1 \iff t^2 < 2(w+1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Fermat halts on the first trial iff balanced]\label{thm:fermat1}
With $p+q=2u$, $q=p+2v$, $v>0$:
\[
\lfloor\sqrt{pq}\rfloor + 1 = u \iff v^2 < 2u \iff (q-p)^2 < 4(p+q).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:sqrtcrit} with $N=pq$, $t=v$, $w+1=u$, legitimate by Lemma~\ref{lem:fid}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Two-sided scan-length bound]\label{thm:excess}
For real $p,q>0$,
\[
\frac{(q-p)^2}{8\max(p,q)} \ \le\ \frac{p+q}{2}-\sqrt{pq} \ \le\ \frac{(q-p)^2}{8\sqrt{pq}} .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Put $x=\sqrt p$, $y=\sqrt q$. The middle term is $\tfrac12(x-y)^2$ and $(q-p)^2 = (y-x)^2(x+y)^2$. The upper bound reduces to $4xy \le (x+y)^2$, i.e.\ $(x-y)^2 \ge 0$; the lower bound reduces to $(x+y)^2 \le 4\max(x^2,y^2)$, which holds since $x^2,y^2 \le \max(x^2,y^2)$.
\end{proof}

Thus the scan length is $\Theta\!\bigl((q-p)^2/\sqrt N\bigr)$: quadratically small in the imbalance, and free at the balance point.

\section{The quartic barrier}\label{sec:barrier}

\begin{theorem}[Quartic barrier, sorted form]\label{thm:quartic}
Let $0 \le a \le b$, $0 \le c \le d$ with $a^2+b^2 = c^2+d^2 = N$ and $d<b$. Then $2N < c^4$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $d<b$ are integers, $b \ge d+1$, so
\[
c^2 = a^2+b^2-d^2 \ \ge\ b^2-d^2 \ \ge\ (d+1)^2-d^2 = 2d+1 .
\]
Hence $c^4 \ge (2d+1)^2 > 4d^2$. On the other hand $c \le d$ gives $2N = 2c^2+2d^2 \le 4d^2$. Chaining, $2N \le 4d^2 < c^4$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Gap-refined barrier]\label{thm:gap}
Under the same sorted hypotheses, if $d+k \le b$ for an integer $k>0$, then $2k^2N < c^4$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Now $c^2 \ge b^2-d^2 \ge (d+k)^2-d^2 = 2kd+k^2 > 0$, so
$c^4 \ge (2kd+k^2)^2 = 4k^2d^2 + 4k^3d + k^4 > 4k^2d^2$, while $N \le 2d^2$; hence $2k^2N \le 4k^2d^2 < c^4$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Three representations]\label{cor:three}
If $N$ has three sorted representations with strictly decreasing large parts $b_3<b_2<b_1$, then $8N < a_3^4$ for the small part $a_3$ of the shallowest.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
$b_3+2 \le b_1$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:gap} with $k=2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Symmetrised form]\label{thm:sym}
If $(a,b)$ and $(c,d)$ are essentially distinct sorted representations of $N$ with non-negative parts, then $2N < \max(a,c)^4$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Essential distinctness forces $b \neq d$ (else $a^2=c^2$ and, the parts being non-negative, the representations coincide). If $d<b$, Theorem~\ref{thm:quartic} gives $2N<c^4 \le \max(a,c)^4$; if $b<d$, apply it with the roles reversed.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Search lower bound]\label{thm:searchlb}
Let $a,b,c,d>0$ with $a^2+b^2 = c^2+d^2 = N$ and the two representations essentially distinct. If $\min(a,b)\le t$ and $\min(c,d)\le t$, then $2N<t^4$: any search that has located the smaller part of each representation has run past $(2N)^{1/4}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:sym}, applied in each of the four order cases, gives $2N < \max(\min(a,b),\min(c,d))^4$; that maximum is non-negative and at most $t$.
\end{proof}

No primality, no genericity, no averaging over instances is used: two distinct lattice points on the circle $x^2+y^2=N$ simply cannot both lie near the axes.

\subsection{Assembling the comparison}

\begin{theorem}[Euler loses on balanced instances]\label{thm:loses}
Let $p \neq q$ be primes $\equiv 1 \pmod 4$, with midpoint $u = (p+q)/2$ and half-gap $v = (q-p)/2>0$, and suppose $v^2 < 2u$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Fermat's difference-of-squares scan succeeds on its first trial: $\lfloor\sqrt{pq}\rfloor + 1 = u$;
\item $pq$ has exactly two essentially distinct positive representations $(A,B)$, $(C,D)$, and any search bound $t$ with $\min(A,B)\le t$, $\min(C,D)\le t$ satisfies $2pq < t^4$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Part 1 is Theorem~\ref{thm:fermat1}; part 2 combines Theorem~\ref{thm:two} with Theorem~\ref{thm:searchlb}.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}[Non-vacuity]
$N = 13\cdot 17 = 221$ is balanced: $u=15$, $v=2$, $v^2 = 4 < 30 = 2u$. Fermat's first trial $s=15$ gives $15^2-221 = 4 = 2^2$: one step. The representations are $221 = 5^2+14^2 = 10^2+11^2$; the larger small part is $10$, and $2\cdot 221 = 442 < 10^4$, consistent with the floor $(442)^{1/4}\approx 4.585$.
\end{example}

\section{The numerical picture}\label{sec:num}

\textbf{Eligibility is exactly the $(1,1)$ cell.} Enumerating semiprimes $pq$ with $p<q$ odd primes below a bound and tabulating by $(p \bmod 4, q \bmod 4)$, the count of normalised representations is $2$ in every instance of the $(1,1)$ cell and $0$ in every instance of the $(1,3)$ and $(3,3)$ cells. The empirical density of primes $\equiv 1 \bmod 4$ among odd primes below $2\cdot 10^5$ is $0.4992$, giving a predicted eligible fraction $0.2492$ against the limit $1/4$.

\textbf{Extraction never fails.} Across large batches of eligible semiprimes, both synthetic and searched, the combination step returns a proper nontrivial divisor in every instance and the two gcds multiply to $N$ in every instance --- as Theorem~\ref{thm:extract} and Theorem~\ref{thm:pair} guarantee. The identities of Lemma~\ref{lem:exactcross} hold exactly on every tested pair, and the extracted primes are the predicted ones.

\textbf{Cost.} On randomly drawn eligible semiprimes the representation search costs a small multiple of one Fermat scan; since Euler needs both representations, the end-to-end comparison is worse again, with a median several times Fermat's cost and a heavy upper tail. The tail is exactly the balanced corner of Theorem~\ref{thm:loses}: for $N = 10009 \times 10037 \approx 10^8$, Fermat halts immediately while the representation search must descend to depth $4867$ --- a ratio unbounded as $N$ grows. Precise multipliers depend on the instance family and on implementation details; the quartic barrier does not.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the results say about the method}

\emph{Correctness is not the problem.} Theorem~\ref{thm:extract} is unconditional, boundary-inclusive, and --- on the canonical pair --- deterministic down to naming which prime each gcd returns (Theorem~\ref{thm:determ}). Given two representations, the method is optimal: two gcds and done.

\emph{Eligibility is a hard quarter.} Theorem~\ref{thm:dich} is an exact classification by residue class, with the count pinned at exactly two (Theorem~\ref{thm:two}). There is no ``mostly works'' regime and no tuning that enlarges the class.

\emph{Cost is where the method dies}, and for a structural reason: Theorem~\ref{thm:searchlb} constrains the target set rather than the search strategy, so no sieve or enumeration order evades it.

\subsection{The geometric reading}

Theorem~\ref{thm:quartic} is a repulsion statement for lattice points on a circle: two distinct lattice points on the circle of radius $\sqrt N$ cannot both lie within angle roughly $(2/N)^{1/4}$ of a coordinate axis. Theorem~\ref{thm:gap} quantifies this: pushing the large parts $k$ apart pushes the shallower small part out by a factor $\sqrt k$. The proof is entirely elementary --- integrality of $b-d \ge 1$ is the only input.

\subsection{Relation to the difference-of-squares route}

Fermat's method succeeds when the factors are close; the representation route is indifferent to the gap but expensive to feed. Theorems~\ref{thm:fermat1} and~\ref{thm:loses} show the two profiles are anti-correlated in the worst possible way: exactly where Fermat is instantaneous, the representation route pays a quartic-depth toll. On the unbalanced side the representation search is long as well, so no regime of advantage exists.

\subsection{Limitations}

The lower bound is stated for searches that locate representations by their small parts, the natural formulation for a scan. It does not rule out obtaining representations by an entirely different route --- e.g.\ by first factoring $N$ (circular) or via Cornacchia's algorithm applied to a square root of $-1$ modulo each prime factor (also circular). Likewise Corollary~\ref{cor:dens} concerns independent draws from the odd primes, whereas the residue-class classification of Theorem~\ref{thm:dich} is instance-by-instance exact regardless.

\section{Future directions}\label{sec:future}

\textbf{Beyond semiprimes.} Theorems~\ref{thm:extract} and~\ref{thm:searchlb} are unconditional on the structure of $N$; only Section~\ref{sec:elig} is semiprime-specific. Extending the classification to $N$ with $k$ prime factors --- where the count is $2^{k-1}$ for squarefree $N$ with all prime factors $\equiv 1 \bmod 4$ --- should combine with Corollary~\ref{cor:three} to give a barrier of the form $c \gg (2^kN)^{1/4}$ for collecting all representations.

\textbf{Sharpening the barrier.} The proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:quartic} discards $a^2$ and the slack $4d+1$. Measured depths (e.g.\ $4867$ against a floor of $119$ for $N \approx 10^8$) suggest the truth is nearer $N^{1/2}$ on average. A matching typical-case lower bound would complete the picture.

\textbf{The class map as a Frobenius invariant.} Theorem~\ref{thm:gauss} and Corollary~\ref{cor:frob} identify each class bit with a choice of Gaussian prime. Phrasing the class map as a homomorphism into ideal-class data over $\Z[i]/(N)$, and asking the analogous question over $\Z[\sqrt{-d}]$ where the class group is nontrivial, is the natural continuation.

\textbf{Other quadratic forms.} The same rigidity argument should run for $x^2+dy^2$ when the form is the principal form of a one-class discriminant. Where the class number exceeds one, ``two representations by the same form'' and ``two representations by forms in the same genus'' split apart, and the extraction step may fail informatively.

\textbf{Algorithmic corollaries.} Theorem~\ref{thm:determ} says which prime the subtractive branch returns as a function of the sign of $BC$. Given a partial representation, this may permit a targeted second search --- capped, however, at the $(2N)^{1/4}$ floor of Theorem~\ref{thm:searchlb}.

